  "response": "Project STAR was a randomized class-size experiment conducted in Tennessee public schools. It was commissioned by the state legislature to settle a question that observational studies had left unresolved. In those studies, schools with smaller classes also tended to differ in funding, staffing and the families they served, so their higher scores could not be credited to class size alone. Project STAR began in the 1985\u201386 school year, and its value for the class-size debate rested entirely on whether its design could separate the effect of class size from the circumstances surrounding it.\n\nBecause it was a randomized experiment in Tennessee public schools, the experiment was carried out during the early 1970s, whereas its longitudinal component followed students from kindergarten through third grade. Its randomized character took a concrete institutional form. Specifically, students and teachers in Project STAR were randomly assigned to class types within each participating school, which meant that comparisons between small and regular classes were made among children who shared the same building, principal and local population. Under this within-school design, any systematic difference in later achievement could in principle be attributed to the class type itself and not to the selection of particular schools or pupils.\n\nThe integrity of that comparison depended on the sample remaining intact across grades. After the experiment's opening year, a large share of students who entered Project STAR left the sample before third grade, and new entrants replaced many of them along the way. Movement within schools raised a further issue. Either some Project STAR students switched class types after their initial assignment, or no Project STAR student switched class types after their initial assignment; these two descriptions of the sample cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Researchers therefore had to consider two distinct threats to the raw estimates. At least one of the following was at work, perhaps both: nonrandom attrition biased the raw comparisons between small and regular classes, and that attrition is what produced the heavy departures from the sample; or student movement between class types biased the raw comparisons between small and regular classes.\n\nAlthough student movement between class types threatened the validity of the simple contrasts, Krueger used students' initial class assignment as an instrumental variable for actual class size. In other words, his analysis treated the randomly assigned starting class type as an instrument for the class size students actually attended. The logic of this strategy is that the original lottery remains uncorrelated with family background and prior ability, even if later moves are not. The instrument therefore isolates the portion of variation in class size that the experiment itself generated. Krueger supplemented this approach by imputing scores for departed students and by examining how the characteristics of leavers differed across class types. This allowed him to bound the plausible influence of the missing observations on the estimated gains.\n\nKrueger found that the small-class achievement gains disappeared once attrition was accounted for. By contrast, Krueger's 1999 analysis concluded that the small-class gains were not driven by nonrandom attrition or class switching. That conclusion settles the tension created by the switching problem: the instrument restored the comparability that random assignment had originally established, and the estimated benefit of smaller classes survived the correction. Because Krueger reached this conclusion, Frederick Mosteller's 1995 review concluded that Project STAR's class-size effects reflected a genuine benefit of smaller classes. Mosteller placed the Tennessee results among the most persuasive large-scale experiments in American education. Together, these assessments established the evidentiary standard for the experiment. A claim that STAR's gains are artifacts of its sample must explain why estimates anchored to the original random assignment yield the same pattern as the raw comparisons. Absent such an explanation, the more defensible reading is that reduced class size, and not differential departure or reassignment, accounts for the higher achievement observed in the small classes.",
